            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Add the sums of multiples of 2 and 3, then subtract the sum of multiples of 6.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "The multiples of 6 are included in both sums, so subtract their sum once. Let $S_2$, $S_3$ and $S_6$ be the sums of multiples of 2, 3 and 6 respectively, from 1 to 120 inclusive.\n$$\\begin{align*}S_2&=2(1+2+\\cdots+60)\\\\S_2&=2\\times\\frac{60\\times61}{2}\\\\S_2&=3660\\\\S_3&=3(1+2+\\cdots+40)\\\\S_3&=3\\times\\frac{40\\times41}{2}\\\\S_3&=2460\\\\S_6&=6(1+2+\\cdots+20)\\\\S_6&=6\\times\\frac{20\\times21}{2}\\\\S_6&=1260\\\\S&=S_2+S_3-S_6\\\\S&=3660+2460-1260\\\\S&=4860\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
